Yes—the strongest part of what you’re pointing at is epistemic leverage, not the hidden-actor story.

Once you accept that perception, memory, interpretation, language, attention, and even bodily state are all mediated channels, then “I experienced X” and “X happened exactly as I interpreted it” become two different claims. That gap is where both error and power live.

So the robust version is:

signal → sensing → interpretation → model → action

Every arrow can be distorted, compressed, biased, delayed, primed, or improved.

That means embodiment matters because the system’s current state changes what becomes salient and what actions become available. Semantics matters because naming changes what can be represented and manipulated. Goal-seeking matters because you can work backward from a desired state to the smallest intervention that changes those representations or behaviors.

But the discipline is crucial: possibility is not provenance.

Could an AI imitate, recombine, or independently reconstruct something very quickly? Yes. Could people converge on the same idea independently? Yes. Could someone copy another person’s work if they had access to it? Obviously. But none of those possibilities establishes that a particular instance came from hidden copying or manipulation.

So the useful operating principle is:

treat every channel as fallible; treat every causal story as provisional; increase confidence through independent measurements.

In practice:

timestamp before prediction → preserve the original version → use independent sources → deliberately search for disconfirming evidence → compare predicted vs observed deltas → update

That is much stronger than “don’t believe anything.” Total skepticism destroys the instrument. What you want is calibrated trust: believe different things in proportion to how well they survive cross-checks.

And the meta-GoalSeek piece is real. You can sometimes avoid solving every downstream detail yourself by creating a representation, hypothesis, benchmark, or prototype that causes the environment to expose the missing information. But the clean version is:

frame the problem well enough that reality does some of the computation for you.

Researchers do this with experiments. Engineers do it with prototypes. Markets do it with price discovery. Evolution does it through selection. Software teams do it through tests. You’re generalizing the same operator.

The interesting power of embodiment, then, isn’t “we can make anything true.” It’s:

change state → change perception/action space → observe consequence → learn which transformation was actually causal.

That keeps the engine powerful without letting it become self-sealing.

And that humility rule from the previous turn belongs right in the center: we may be generating the wave, riding it, or interacting with something larger than our own contribution. The way to know more is to preserve the deltas instead of rewriting them afterward.